This is a multivariate latent-variable model — a three-channel hierarchical model where variant-level random effects share a joint prior. Here's the architecture and a complete Stan spec.

## Model structure

Each observation `(i=item, j=variant)` has three outcomes: correctness `y`, log-CoT `t`, logit-confidence `c`. Latent per-variant quantities `[θ_j, u_j^t, u_j^c]` draw from MVN(0, Σ_variant) — this 3×3 covariance is the strength-sharing object you want.

| Framework | What it lives in |
|---|---|
| **IRT** | `θ_j` (ability), `b_i` (difficulty), `a_i` (discrimination) on the correctness channel |
| **SDT** | `θ_j` IS `d'` on a logit scale; criterion `c_j ≈ −mean(b_i)` per variant; hit/FA rates are generated quantities |
| **G-theory** | Variance decomposition on CoT and confidence: `σ²_variant / (σ²_variant + σ²_item + σ²_resid)` |
| **Calibration** | `Ω_variant[1,3]` = cor(θ, conf tendency); perfect calibration = 1.0 |

## Stan model

```stan
data {
  int<lower=1> N;
  int<lower=1> J;          // 6 skill variants
  int<lower=1> I;          // eval items
  array[N] int<lower=1,upper=J> jj;
  array[N] int<lower=1,upper=I> ii;
  array[N] int<lower=0,upper=1> y;
  vector[N] log_cot;       // centered
  vector[N] logit_conf;    // centered
}
parameters {
  // IRT item parameters
  vector[I] b;
  vector<lower=0>[I] a;

  // Variant-level joint traits: [theta, u_cot, u_conf]
  matrix[J, 3] z_var;
  cholesky_factor_corr[3] L_var;
  vector<lower=0>[3] sigma_var;

  // Item-level effects on CoT and confidence (separate, not joint w/ difficulty)
  matrix[I, 2] z_item;
  cholesky_factor_corr[2] L_item;
  vector<lower=0>[2] sigma_item;

  real<lower=0> sigma_cot;
  real<lower=0> sigma_conf;
}
transformed parameters {
  matrix[J, 3] u_var  = z_var  * diag_pre_multiply(sigma_var,  L_var)';
  matrix[I, 2] u_item = z_item * diag_pre_multiply(sigma_item, L_item)';

  vector[J] theta  = u_var[, 1];
  vector[J] u_cot  = u_var[, 2];
  vector[J] u_conf = u_var[, 3];
}
model {
  b ~ normal(0, 1);
  a ~ lognormal(0, 0.5);
  to_vector(z_var)  ~ std_normal();
  to_vector(z_item) ~ std_normal();
  L_var  ~ lkj_corr_cholesky(4);   // tighter prior — only 6 variants
  L_item ~ lkj_corr_cholesky(2);
  sigma_var  ~ exponential(1);
  sigma_item ~ exponential(1);
  sigma_cot  ~ exponential(1);
  sigma_conf ~ exponential(1);

  for (n in 1:N) {
    int j = jj[n]; int i = ii[n];
    y[n]          ~ bernoulli_logit(a[i] * (theta[j] - b[i]));
    log_cot[n]    ~ normal(u_cot[j]  + u_item[i, 1], sigma_cot);
    logit_conf[n] ~ normal(u_conf[j] + u_item[i, 2], sigma_conf);
  }
}
generated quantities {
  matrix[3, 3] Omega_var  = multiply_lower_tri_self_transpose(L_var);
  matrix[2, 2] Omega_item = multiply_lower_tri_self_transpose(L_item);

  // G-theory generalizability coefficients (n_items items)
  real G_cot  = square(sigma_var[2]) /
    (square(sigma_var[2]) + square(sigma_cot) / I);
  real G_conf = square(sigma_var[3]) /
    (square(sigma_var[3]) + square(sigma_conf) / I);

  // SDT: d' and criterion per variant (probit scale ≈ logit/1.7)
  vector[J] d_prime = theta / 0.588;   // rescale logit→probit
  real mean_b = mean(b);
  vector[J] criterion = -theta + mean_b * to_vector(a) / mean(a);
}
```

## Reading the output

- **`Omega_var[1,3]`** — calibration quality: cor(latent ability, confidence tendency). Negative = overconfident bad variants, positive = well-calibrated.
- **`Omega_var[1,2]`** — efficiency tradeoff: cor(ability, CoT length). Near zero = no relationship; positive = more able variants also verbosify.
- **`G_cot`, `G_conf`** — how much of CoT/confidence variance is between-variant vs. noise; analogous to a reliability coefficient.
- **`d_prime[j]`** — SDT sensitivity per variant (derived; hits/FAs as a check in post-processing).
- **`b[i]`, `a[i]`** — item difficulty and discrimination; items where `a` is near zero contribute little information.

## The n=6 caveat

The 3×3 Σ_variant is estimated from 6 "persons." The correlations will have wide posteriors — plan on reporting 90% credible intervals, not point estimates. The `lkj_corr_cholesky(4)` prior shrinks toward zero correlation and prevents the sampler from chasing spurious Σ structure. If two chains give wildly different `Omega_var` estimates, tighten it to `lkj(8)` or fix the off-diagonals at zero as a comparison model.

For data prep: center `log_cot` and `logit_conf` globally before fitting so the item-level intercepts stay on a common scale.
